WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:03.630
You would not hit someone
with an open chair.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:06.000
That is a huge no-no.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.520
Hi, I'm WWE Superstar Shelton Benjamin.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.380
You may know me as The Gold Standard.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.600
I'm a three-time
Intercontinental champion,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.060
United States champion,
three-time Tag Team champion,

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.370
three-time 24/7 champion.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.295
Today, we're going to be looking

00:00:18.295 --> 00:00:19.895
at wrestling scenes in movies,

00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:22.356
and I'll be judging
just how real they are.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.620
That was a good sequence,
where she grabbed the headlock

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.280
and run up the rope into
the headlock takeover.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.640
Like, that was actually a
really cool, very realistic,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.283
very accurate little sequence of action.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.980
I'm being nitpicky.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.205
When she had her foot on her neck,

00:00:49.205 --> 00:00:51.307
usually a ref would be saying,
"Get the foot off the neck,"

00:00:51.307 --> 00:00:52.873
and they will start counting.

00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:54.330
Because when they start counting,

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.670
if they get to five, you get DQ'd. So.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.390
And that's a super-small, nitpicky thing,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.463
but this was very good.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.810
Great athleticism on
the part of her brother.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.640
I think that was a little
bit of showboating.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.065
He went to the top, but I don't know why.

00:01:11.065 --> 00:01:13.406
And believe it or not,

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:16.655
that happens a lot in pro wrestling.

00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:19.274
Guys will do stuff from the
top rope, and even I will go,

00:01:19.274 --> 00:01:21.515
"Why'd you do that?
Why'd you go up there?"

00:01:21.515 --> 00:01:22.848
"Oh, because it looked cool." OK.

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:24.810
It ain't about the size
of the dog in the fight.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:26.370
It's about the size of
the fight in the dog,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.340
and this dog is about to bite hard.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.960
I heard it 20 years ago.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.832
Those are like lines you
could get off a Dixie cup.

00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:34.548
Remember, wrestling,

00:01:34.548 --> 00:01:36.398
to me, that's, like, 20% of what we do,

00:01:36.398 --> 00:01:37.890
because it's storytelling.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.020
You have to learn how to talk.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.490
You have to learn how to carry yourself.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.120
So that stuff, yes, you want
to practice that all the time.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.860
You want to practice
that whenever you can.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.080
I started with John Cena,
and I remember asking him,

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.010
how did you get so good at
what we call cutting promos?

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.177
And John said, I basically
just sit at home all day

00:01:56.177 --> 00:01:57.690
and talk to myself in the mirror.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.398
And that's a part of
wrestling that I think

00:01:59.398 --> 00:02:02.370
a lot of aspiring wrestlers don't get.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.670
They assume it's the moves.
It's never the moves.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.628
I've seen a million elbow drops,

00:02:07.628 --> 00:02:10.469
but for some reason, when I
see the Macho Man does it,

00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:11.700
it's something special.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.180
It's the personalities that
surround all of these moves.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.315
The moves are actually secondary.

00:02:17.315 --> 00:02:19.110
From the setting to the
moves to the energy,

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.213
this is definitely a 10 out of 10 for me.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.290
In lucha-style wrestling, you
do have the ring full of guys,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.850
super-outlandish characters,
doing some really unique moves.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.830
You would think it's like a battle royal,

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.510
just a big game of king of the mountain.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.060
You got a lot of guys
moving at the same time,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.140
a lot of moving parts.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.630
I didn't see a lot of bad stuff.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.450
I saw things I would expect
to see at a lucha show.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:51.483
The only thing that I really saw

00:02:51.483 --> 00:02:53.649
that I wouldn't like

00:02:53.649 --> 00:02:57.960
is so many guys doing so
much stuff in a small space.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.513
There's just not enough room

00:02:59.513 --> 00:03:01.988
to safely bounce around like that.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.750
The moves themselves, very good.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.940
If you ever watch, you
see some amazing things,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.230
even things that I go, wow,

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.880
I didn't even know you could do that.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.670
So even when the guy has
the guys in two headlocks,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.095
that one's obviously rigged,

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:20.040
but I feel like I've seen so
much lucha stuff that I go,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.960
I have no idea how they're doing that.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.833
That doesn't actually make
me think that's impossible.

00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:27.392
I feel like there are guys that
can actually pull that off.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.970
Outside interference.
That's totally illegal.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.070
You're not allowed to do it, but hey,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.950
what the ref doesn't see ...

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:41.940
It's just that simple.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.822
As long as the ref
doesn't see it, you're OK.

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:46.200
WWE, if the ref sees it, you'll get DQ'd.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.030
And I don't mean you'll get DQ'd

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.380
because that's in the script.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.760
I mean you'll get DQ'd

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.070
because that's what the
ref is supposed to do.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:56.462
If you are going to have
those kind of situations,

00:03:56.462 --> 00:03:58.003
better make sure the ref isn't looking.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.860
He gave a perfect tombstone
piledriver in the end.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.693
It was safe.

00:04:08.693 --> 00:04:10.590
You notice the guy's head
didn't touch the mat.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:11.810
But the tombstone piledriver,

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.560
this was made famous by The Undertaker.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.150
From a safety aspect, this
would actually kill a man.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.691
To protect yourself,
the guy taking the move

00:04:20.691 --> 00:04:24.133
would wrap his arms around
the guy giving its waist,

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.375
and the guy giving it would not

00:04:26.375 --> 00:04:28.890
drive his face into the floor.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:30.598
Most moves, they have a name,

00:04:30.598 --> 00:04:33.081
but when associated with certain talent,

00:04:33.081 --> 00:04:34.230
they name it after themselves.

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:37.620
The tombstone piledriver
is specifically named

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:39.480
because it's The Undertaker's.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.180
I would probably give that a 9.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.837
We call it hitting the ropes.

00:04:45.837 --> 00:04:48.000
Basically just running and
bouncing off the ropes.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:48.960
There are two kinds of ropes.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.450
There are cable, where
there's actually a steel cable

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.610
with a rubber casing around it,

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.440
and there's rope. Real rope.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.970
Actually, it's very springy.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.538
It's actually cheaper
to have, but for WWE,

00:05:00.538 --> 00:05:02.529
we use real rope because real rope,

00:05:02.529 --> 00:05:04.387
you get the best results all around.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.930
It works great for
catapulting you off the ring.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.716
And it's more comfy.

00:05:08.716 --> 00:05:10.133
Tag me! Tag me!

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:16.440
So, tag matches, you
have two or more partners

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.540
versus another team, two or more partners.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.330
One guy stays on the outside
while the two in the ring,

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:22.290
they battle it out.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.770
And eventually the other
guy literally tags out.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.690
That's pretty realistic.
We'll be on the ring.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.150
We'll be reaching, going,
"Come on, come on, get to me."

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.223
And it's all part of the storytelling

00:05:32.223 --> 00:05:33.690
to help build drama.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:35.160
I'm a three-time Tag Team champion.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.260
So I've done it quite a few times.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.160
And in a lot of cases, it's a
good chance to catch a rest.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.930
There is absolutely
bells in pro wrestling.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:50.580
Start the match, finish the match.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.220
After every match, you'll
see the ref go ring the bell.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:54.078
You see a ref do that,

00:05:54.078 --> 00:05:55.661
that's their "ring the bell, it's over,"

00:05:55.661 --> 00:05:57.660
or "ring the bell, it starts."

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.730
Most people aren't paying
attention to the ref,

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:01.080
so they don't catch that little thing.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.660
But there is an actual
bell next to the ring,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:04.800
next to the announcers.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.322
While it's very entertaining,

00:06:06.322 --> 00:06:09.280
I would have to give
this a pretty low score.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.113
A 5.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.830
I'm actually amazed at the fact

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:24.330
that this is very, very accurate.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.143
Every move is pretty much spot-on.

00:06:27.143 --> 00:06:28.267
And I guess it would be,

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:31.042
considering you have Hulk
Hogan giving the moves.

00:06:31.042 --> 00:06:33.365
Like, one of the biggest names
in pro-wrestling history.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.400
The only thing that I
would say wouldn't happen

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.590
is the girls on the apron
while the match is going on,

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.140
because that's a safety hazard.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.100
We don't actually use chairs like that.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.690
We actually use just steel chairs.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.540
Wood tends to splinter. It's dangerous.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.400
You get cut. We just don't use it.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.260
I would not allow that in a match I'm in.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.780
You would not hit someone
with an open chair.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:08.163
That is a huge no-no. Too many
bad things can happen there.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.340
He could have based a little better.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.200
Get a little more height on that hip toss.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.970
Stallone could have positioned
himself to get thrown higher.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.230
He muscled him over.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.663
There is an art to taking a beating.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:25.740
Wow. So, stop.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:27.048
If you look right here,

00:07:27.048 --> 00:07:29.806
that is a example of what we call posting.

00:07:29.806 --> 00:07:31.680
See where his hands are?

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.771
He's posting. He's
helping him pick him up.

00:07:33.771 --> 00:07:36.096
But that hand in the back helps him

00:07:36.096 --> 00:07:37.650
control him and move around.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:40.020
It looks like a small thing,
but it's a huge thing.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.536
He's definitely holding
all of that weight,

00:07:42.536 --> 00:07:44.202
but it's easier because he has

00:07:44.202 --> 00:07:45.870
three points of carrying the weight,

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.240
which makes everything
easier for everyone.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.263
It helps everyone, and
it's actually safer.

00:07:53.445 --> 00:07:54.753
Timber!

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:59.616
He took the safest bump out of the ring

00:07:59.616 --> 00:08:00.810
that you could possibly take.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.098
And bump just means when he falls.

00:08:03.098 --> 00:08:05.040
Kind of hooked the top
rope to kill the momentum

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:06.360
and just kind of spun out of it.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:11.040
From a beginner standpoint,
this is all very, very good.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.080
It was definitely a more '80s style.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.385
Maybe even '70s, again,
because you have the fans

00:08:15.385 --> 00:08:17.795
so close to the ring.
But between the moves,

00:08:17.795 --> 00:08:20.430
the execution, the selling, the posting,

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:23.040
everything about this to me is real.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.260
This is a 10 out of 10 for me.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.370
Peter: Take the chain off!

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:30.840
Hey, freakshow! You're going nowhere.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.770
I got you for three minutes.

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.560
This is awesome.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.030
The cage itself, those
bars look pretty thick

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.230
and pretty flimsy.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:43.500
It felt more like Thunderdome
than a cage match.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:46.830
Typically we use a cage
match for bigger stories.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:49.020
It's kind of like the last big battle,

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:50.730
a long rivalry between two talents.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.200
So that's a pretty big deal.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.780
And usually it wouldn't
be your first match.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:58.002
And three minutes for pro wrestling,

00:08:58.002 --> 00:09:01.150
if you put a cage down and
you only go three minutes,

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:03.480
yeah, you might have
a riot from the crowd.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.340
The actual length of a
cage match would vary.

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:09.660
I would say no less than 15 minutes,

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.940
just because it's usually the main event

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:13.323
of whatever event it is.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:21.360
If you had asked me this maybe
when this movie was made,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.840
I would say Spider-Man moves
are pretty unrealistic.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.300
But then I have to point
out guys like Ricochet,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.033
who can literally do anything

00:09:30.033 --> 00:09:31.812
that you've seen in this clip.

00:09:31.812 --> 00:09:34.413
This day and age, I can't
say they're unrealistic.

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:38.820
He knows how to get it, the chair shot.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.310
In this clip, Macho Man plays Bone Saw.

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:45.270
"Macho Man" Randy Savage
is one of the greatest

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:48.778
professional-wrestlers-slash-sports-entertainers

00:09:48.778 --> 00:09:49.653
of all time.

00:09:49.653 --> 00:09:51.420
During the '80s and early '90s,

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.250
there was no one bigger.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.113
This is great work for Macho Man.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.340
What we don't do is we don't
take objects from the crowd

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.880
to hit each other with.
That's a big no-no.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:05.130
If you start passing stuff into the ring,

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:07.050
well, you're probably
going to get ejected.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:11.280
If it were a manager or valet or partner,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:12.300
that's a completely different story.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.992
But generally, we're
not just going to take

00:10:13.992 --> 00:10:15.750
a random object from the crowd.

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:18.000
I have seen guys take food
from the crowd, though.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.040
Like, if you got a bag
of popcorn or a drink,

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.830
I've seen guys grab that and
throw it in each other's face.

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.658
I'm going to give this one an 8.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:32.328
Kia, fans know her as
Awesome Kong, she's actually

00:10:32.328 --> 00:10:36.845
a very, very, very famous pro wrestler.

00:10:36.845 --> 00:10:38.970
She had a small stint in WWE.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:40.712
In Japan, she's legend.

00:10:40.712 --> 00:10:43.263
But you can totally see
she knows pro wrestling.

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:51.750
They're doing a great job.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:52.980
They're really taking their time

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.990
and doing actual wrestling moves.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:56.850
So, move-wise,

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.740
yes, they are spot-on with the move.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:02.310
They're definitely slowing
down for the actresses,

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:04.170
because a lot of those moves,
it happens a lot faster.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:05.383
I don't have a problem with that

00:11:05.383 --> 00:11:07.574
because, again, they're doing a show.

00:11:07.574 --> 00:11:09.290
When we get to the actual wrestling,

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:11.998
I actually think, compared
to the actual "GLOW"

00:11:11.998 --> 00:11:13.031
that I watched as a kid,

00:11:13.031 --> 00:11:15.406
they're pretty toned down, in my opinion,

00:11:15.406 --> 00:11:17.490
because "GLOW" was wild.

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:21.257
That power of Christ compels you!

00:11:25.446 --> 00:11:26.279
I like this.

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:28.800
Character work in between
moves and showmanship.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.830
These are all things that
pro wrestlers actually do.

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:33.600
I feel like there's an art to catchphrase.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:35.730
The Undertaker had "rest in peace."

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:37.170
It's a way to connect with the fans.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:39.750
And the best thing is when they
start doing it back at you.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:40.890
There's nothing better.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.290
You know you're doing the right thing

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:45.330
when the fans start mimicking you.

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:48.930
The ring looks real. The moves look real.

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:51.030
The character work is great.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.700
Everything about this
really does remind me

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.070
of "GLOW" that I used to watch as a kid.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:59.700
I would sit up and watch
"GLOW" every Saturday night.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.290
I'm giving it a 10 out of 10.

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.030
They didn't turn out the
way that I had envisioned.

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:04.542
I'm getting more of a chicken vibe.

00:12:04.542 --> 00:12:06.237
So, we're birdmen, we're birds of war.

00:12:06.237 --> 00:12:07.387
On the amateur level, yeah,

00:12:07.387 --> 00:12:09.900
people definitely decide
what their characters are,

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.390
what their attire is,
what their shtick is.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:15.060
We call it ring gear. On any given night,

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.490
I've always picked my own ring gear.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:18.586
You think you're so tough, huh?

00:12:18.586 --> 00:12:20.789
Ah! Stop throwing sand!

00:12:20.789 --> 00:12:22.111
Shelton: This is how you get canceled.

00:12:22.111 --> 00:12:23.352
Mac: You're just going
to throw sand in my eyes,

00:12:23.352 --> 00:12:24.235
so I'm going to run away.

00:12:24.235 --> 00:12:25.290
Shelton: It's very over the top.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:28.560
The setting of this is definitely
of a small indie company.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.604
You might be prone to see

00:12:30.604 --> 00:12:34.110
guys who are less
experienced on indie scenes.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:35.898
I've seen it where guys are just

00:12:35.898 --> 00:12:39.690
way too into their characters
and go way over the top,

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:42.870
and they basically tick
off the other performers

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:45.390
till some of them just
refuse to work with them.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.690
I mean, you will see,
just like the one guy,

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:49.880
"I think if I get in
the ring you're going to

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:51.985
throw salt in my face, so
I'm just going to leave.

00:12:51.985 --> 00:12:55.440
So, I mean, guys have walked
out on matches before.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:58.260
As funny as this is, there's
a lot of accuracy in it.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:12:59.703
I'm going to say a 6.

00:13:01.096 --> 00:13:03.155
Is that pro wrestling?

00:13:08.016 --> 00:13:09.724
Geez.

00:13:09.724 --> 00:13:13.320
That looked more like Bane
breaking Batman's back.

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:15.011
Some "Dark Knight" stuff.

00:13:15.011 --> 00:13:16.503
That backbreaker she gave,

00:13:16.503 --> 00:13:20.280
that's definitely not a
pro-wrestling-style backbreaker.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:21.878
The difference is there's no way

00:13:21.878 --> 00:13:24.036
to protect the guy from that.

00:13:24.036 --> 00:13:26.550
She dropped him right on her
knee in the small of his back.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.820
If it were done, you actually
would break someone's back.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:31.595
So when I bring a guy down,

00:13:31.595 --> 00:13:34.053
I don't want to bring him down
on the small of your back,

00:13:34.053 --> 00:13:35.400
because I don't want you to bend.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.177
What I want to do is maneuver you

00:13:37.177 --> 00:13:39.393
closer to across your shoulders,

00:13:39.393 --> 00:13:40.659
between the shoulder blades,

00:13:40.659 --> 00:13:42.603
so there's no pressure on your back.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:47.081
The way she came,

00:13:47.081 --> 00:13:48.914
I wouldn't even call
it a torpedo dropkick,

00:13:48.914 --> 00:13:50.610
because it looked like she
jumped off of something

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:52.590
and just plowed.

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:55.475
The crazy thing is that is
exactly what it feels like

00:13:55.475 --> 00:13:57.492
when someone gives you a dropkick,

00:13:57.492 --> 00:13:59.191
particularly off the top rope.

00:13:59.191 --> 00:14:01.392
I've seen guys running and do it,

00:14:01.392 --> 00:14:03.083
but not with that kind of velocity.

00:14:03.083 --> 00:14:07.866
But you really are getting
basically kicked in the chest,

00:14:07.866 --> 00:14:11.140
and there is no way to fake that.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:14.280
Now my professional opinion,
OK, I would say sell it more.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.920
You're right back up? Man,
what are you, Superman?

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:18.912
But the actual dropkick itself,

00:14:18.912 --> 00:14:20.503
that looked really legit.

00:14:20.503 --> 00:14:22.740
I would give the backbreaker a 3,

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.582
and I would give the dropkick a 10.

00:14:27.009 --> 00:14:28.780
They're just drilling.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:30.697
This is basic drilling.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:33.300
I was a collegiate wrestler.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:34.850
There are little things that I can tell

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.441
as an actor that he doesn't know.

00:14:36.441 --> 00:14:38.900
Little things, like once
you get into a guy's leg,

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:39.947
you know, popping your head up

00:14:39.947 --> 00:14:41.100
so that you can go around him.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:43.650
Because if you're looking
down, you're easy to take down.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:45.300
It's like the body follows the head.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:46.710
So I don't keep my head down.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.387
Every amateur wrestler knows this,

00:14:48.387 --> 00:14:49.853
and it makes it easier to go around.

00:14:49.853 --> 00:14:51.069
Just little things.

00:14:51.069 --> 00:14:53.190
I feel like I would
defend him rather easily,

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.840
because his head keeps going down.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:56.430
But it looks very accurate.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:59.900
With WWE, when we lock up,
we don't put our heads down

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:02.640
because, again, we're performing.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.470
If we locked up, if we
both put our head down,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.880
well, we can't see each other.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.853
And a lot of cases, you
get accidental headbutts.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.090
This is during a sparring.

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:18.390
They did a great job of
depicting how Schultz moved.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:21.600
His mannerisms, the
intensity, having hand fights.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.280
Your hands are your defense.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.230
Some people might say
first line of defense.

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:26.640
Some people might say second line.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.320
It kind of varies with who I'm wrestling.

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:31.320
They're actually doing a very good job

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.136
of depicting the style
Schultz would wrestle,

00:15:34.136 --> 00:15:35.411
because if you watch in his tapes,

00:15:35.411 --> 00:15:37.290
his arms are always kind of T.-rex-y.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:39.060
When I wrestled, my hands were forward,

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:40.800
because I wanted to catch everything.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:43.380
I feel like what they're doing
is kind of a Russian style,

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:45.060
because it looks very relaxed.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:46.737
One of my teammates was a Russian.

00:15:46.737 --> 00:15:48.720
He actually fought in UFC a few times.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:51.270
Vladimir Matyushenko. And
when he would wrestle me,

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.760
he had the same T.-rex-y-style arms.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:54.697
Like, some people, you'd grab them,

00:15:54.697 --> 00:15:55.846
they'd get stiff and everything,

00:15:55.846 --> 00:15:59.230
but Vladimir, when I would grab
him, he would stay relaxed.

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.540
And that messed with me,
because my thinking is

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:05.310
if someone grabs you, your
first reaction is to pull away,

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:06.480
but he did nothing.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.090
And it was kind of like, OK,
what do you do with this?

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:12.510
So I think they're depicting
what Dave Schultz does,

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:15.870
not necessarily what an
amateur wrestler does

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.471
or your typical amateur wrestler would do.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:24.570
Wow. He did a little spin move

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:28.478
that I've only ever seen one person do,

00:16:28.478 --> 00:16:29.411
in college.

00:16:29.411 --> 00:16:31.140
And that guy was me.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:33.960
I've never, ever seen
anyone do the standing spin

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:35.400
drop into a double leg.

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:37.503
I'm the only guy that
I knew ever did that.

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:41.310
I think every amateur wrestler
should watch this movie.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:42.717
This is definitely a 10.

00:16:42.717 --> 00:16:44.790
Favorite wrestling movie? I'll be honest.

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:47.372
I'm kind of a fan of "Nacho
Libre." I like the silliness.

00:16:47.372 --> 00:16:51.090
The tombstone. It looked really
convincing, yet really safe.

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:54.212
If you enjoyed this
video, please click above.

